{"id":"security-controlling-access","corpus":"k8s","title":"Controlling Access to the Kubernetes API","sourceUrl":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/security/controlling-access/","license":"Kubernetes Documentation © The Kubernetes Authors, licensed CC BY 4.0","licenseUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/","blocks":[{"kind":"h1","text":"Controlling Access to the Kubernetes API"},{"kind":"p","text":"This page provides an overview of controlling access to the Kubernetes API."},{"kind":"p","text":"Users access the Kubernetes API using kubectl, client libraries, or by making REST requests. Both human users and Kubernetes service accounts can be authorized for API access. When a request reaches the API, it goes through several stages, illustrated in the following diagram:"},{"kind":"h2","text":"Transport security"},{"kind":"p","text":"By default, the Kubernetes API server listens on port 6443 on the first non-localhost network interface, protected by TLS. In a typical production Kubernetes cluster, the API serves on port 443. The port can be changed with the --secure-port, and the listening IP address with the --bind-address flag."},{"kind":"p","text":"The API server presents a certificate. This certificate may be signed using a private certificate authority (CA), or based on a public key infrastructure linked to a generally recognized CA. The certificate and corresponding private key can be set by using the --tls-cert-file and --tls-private-key-file flags."},{"kind":"p","text":"If your cluster uses a private certificate authority, you need a copy of that CA certificate configured into your ~/.kube/config on the client, so that you can trust the connection and be confident it was not intercepted."},{"kind":"p","text":"Your client can present a TLS client certificate at this stage."},{"kind":"h2","text":"Authentication"},{"kind":"p","text":"Once TLS is established, the HTTP request moves to the Authentication step. This is shown as step 1 in the diagram. The cluster creation script or cluster admin configures the API server to run one or more Authenticator modules. Authenticators are described in more detail in Authentication."},{"kind":"p","text":"The input to the authentication step is the entire HTTP request; however, it typically examines the headers and/or client certificate."},{"kind":"p","text":"Authentication modules include client certificates, password, and plain tokens, bootstrap tokens, and JSON Web Tokens (used for service accounts)."},{"kind":"p","text":"Multiple authentication modules can be specified, in which case each one is tried in sequence, until one of them succeeds."},{"kind":"p","text":"If the request cannot be authenticated, it is rejected with HTTP status code 401. Otherwise, the user is authenticated as a specific username, and the user name is available to subsequent steps to use in their decisions. Some authenticators also provide the group memberships of the user, while other authenticators do not."},{"kind":"p","text":"While Kubernetes uses usernames for access control decisions and in request logging, it does not have a User object nor does it store usernames or other information about users in its API."},{"kind":"h2","text":"Authorization"},{"kind":"p","text":"After the request is authenticated as coming from a specific user, the request must be authorized. This is shown as step 2 in the diagram."},{"kind":"p","text":"A request must include the username of the requester, the requested action, and the object affected by the action. The request is authorized if an existing policy declares that the user has permissions to complete the requested action."},{"kind":"p","text":"For example, if Bob has the policy below, then he can read pods only in the namespace projectCaribou:"},{"kind":"p","text":"If Bob makes the following request, the request is authorized because he is allowed to read objects in the projectCaribou namespace:"},{"kind":"p","text":"If Bob makes a request to write (create or update) to the objects in the projectCaribou namespace, his authorization is denied. If Bob makes a request to read (get) objects in a different namespace such as projectFish, then his authorization is denied."},{"kind":"p","text":"Kubernetes authorization requires that you use common REST attributes to interact with existing organization-wide or cloud-provider-wide access control systems. It is important to use REST formatting because these control systems might interact with other APIs besides the Kubernetes API."},{"kind":"p","text":"Kubernetes supports multiple authorization modules, such as ABAC mode, RBAC Mode, and Webhook mode. When an administrator creates a cluster, they configure the authorization modules that should be used in the API server. If more than one authorization modules are configured, Kubernetes checks each module, and if any module authorizes the request, then the request can proceed. If all of the modules deny the request, then the request is denied (HTTP status code 403)."},{"kind":"p","text":"To learn more about Kubernetes authorization, including details about creating policies using the supported authorization modules, see Authorization."},{"kind":"h2","text":"Admission control"},{"kind":"p","text":"Admission Control modules are software modules that can modify or reject requests. In addition to the attributes available to Authorization modules, Admission Control modules can access the contents of the object that is being created or modified."}],"lemmas":{"controlling":["controlling","control"],"controllings":["controllings","controlling"],"controls":["control","controls"],"controlled":["controlled","control"],"accesses":["access","accesses"],"accessed":["accessed","access"],"kubernetes":["kubernetes","kubernete"],"pages":["page","pages"],"paging":["paging","page"],"provides":["provides","provide"],"provided":["provided","provide"],"providing":["providing","provide"],"overviews":["overview","overviews"],"users":["user","users"],"using"